{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pon-05-two-digit-numbers-reversed-digits",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Two-Digit Numbers and Reversed Digits",
    "description": "Use place value to turn digit clues and reversal statements into simple equations, while detecting when the number is not unique.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start from place value, not memorised reversal formulas. Derive the 9-times digit-gap and 11-times digit-sum patterns, then use them in multiple-of-digit-sum, reversal-plus-sum, digit-product and insufficiency questions. The final block combines place value with a nonlinear digit relation. Every solution checks digit limits and the actual reversed number.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "Teach a two-digit number with tens digit x and units digit y as 10x+y, with reverse 10y+x and digit sum x+y. Derive original−reverse = 9(x−y) and original+reverse = 11(x+y) from place value rather than memorising them. Cover a number being a multiple of its digit sum, fixed reversal changes, digit-sum/digit-difference clues, digit products, and nonlinear digit conditions such as the unit digit being the square of the tens digit. Include cases where reversal information determines only the digit difference and therefore the exact number is data inadequate. Respect digit limits throughout."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's make digit puzzles stop feeling mysterious.\n\nA two-digit number is just tens and units with different place values. Once we model those places correctly, reversals become ordinary algebra.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A two-digit number is not 'x plus y'. Place value matters.\n\nIf the tens digit is x and the units digit is y:\n\nNumber = 10x + y",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For 47:\n4 tens = 40\n7 units = 7\n40+7 = 47\n\nReverse the digits and the value becomes 10y+x. For 47, that is 74.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
